WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:01.042
Okay.

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.711
I want you to check this out

00:00:02.711 --> 00:00:05.380
because it represents a big challenge

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.550
for large language models
like GPT-3...

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:11.386
and now GPT-4.

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:14.264
But it is not code.

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:17.392
It is a list that countries 
around the world

00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:18.935
are grappling with.

00:00:20.228 --> 00:00:22.147
Before we get into the problems

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:25.066
with large language models, 
let's review at a basic level...

00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:26.735
how they work.

00:00:27.986 --> 00:00:30.155
You've probably heard 
of ChatGPT.

00:00:30.155 --> 00:00:31.364
It's not really a model

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:34.617
but an app that sits on top of 
a large language model.

00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:37.245
In this case, a version 
of GPT.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:40.582
One thing models like ChatGPT do

00:00:40.582 --> 00:00:42.751
is natural language processing.

00:00:43.126 --> 00:00:44.627
It's used in everything 

00:00:44.627 --> 00:00:47.714
from telephone customer service 
to auto completion.

00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:50.383
GPT is like a used bookstore.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.635
It's never left the room

00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:56.056
and only learned through the books 
that are at the store.

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:59.017
Essentially, these large language models...

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.562
they scan a lot of text and 
try to learn a language.

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:06.024
They can check their process 
by covering up the answers...

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:08.568
and then seeing if they got it right.

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:11.988
Then they can use that knowledge 
to recognize sentiment...

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:14.532
summarize, translate

00:01:14.532 --> 00:01:17.702
and generate responses 
or recommendations

00:01:17.702 --> 00:01:19.829
based on the analyzed data.

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:22.373
And yes, ChatGPT wrote 
that last line.

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:24.584
You've got to do that 
in videos like this.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.169
This is an amazing ability

00:01:26.169 --> 00:01:28.463
but that's because it's read 
 a lot of stuff.

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:30.465
You can ask ChatGPT

00:01:30.465 --> 00:01:32.092
to rephrase something 
to Shakespeare

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:34.844
but that's because it's read 
all the Shakespeare

00:01:34.844 --> 00:01:37.514
and that is where my waste of paper 
comes in.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:40.475
Actually, I want to show 
you something.

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:44.479
Yeah? Good?

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:45.063
Okay.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.775
So this is a print out of Common Crawl

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:51.528
from 2008 to the present.

00:01:51.528 --> 00:01:55.281
Common crawl basically means that 
they go over all the websites

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.699
and index them.

00:01:56.699 --> 00:01:58.785
And on this list, they put 
every language

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.329
that they think that 
they've indexed.

00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:04.124
Here, you notice right away 
all the English.

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:08.294
Every crawl is like more than 
40% just English.

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:10.130
German: DEU.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:11.422
See the indexes here...

00:02:11.422 --> 00:02:13.883
you know, it's about 
6% every time

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:17.137
which doesn't sound like a lot, 
but it's kind of a lot.

00:02:18.513 --> 00:02:20.932
But look here, 2023.

00:02:20.932 --> 00:02:24.060
FIN: Finnish. Lot of pages.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.647
But it's just 0.4% of the entire scan.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:31.317
This bookstore, it's got 
an inventory problem.

00:02:31.526 --> 00:02:35.947
All of the focus is on only 
a very small set of languages.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:40.493
There was a paper that stated that 
of the 7,000 languages spoken globally

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:42.912
about 20 of those languages

00:02:42.912 --> 00:02:44.956
make up the bulk of NLP research.

00:02:44.956 --> 00:02:46.416
Okay, so let's back up a bit.

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:48.251
This is Ruth-Ann Armstrong.

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:51.421
She's a researcher who I interviewed 
and she's doing something

00:02:51.421 --> 00:02:53.715
that a lot of researchers 
are trying to do...

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:56.384
make new data sets.

00:02:56.384 --> 00:02:58.887
Those 20 languages fall into 
our category called

00:02:58.887 --> 00:03:00.388
high-resource languages

00:03:00.388 --> 00:03:02.182
and the others fall 
into a category

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:03.892
called low-resource languages.

00:03:03.892 --> 00:03:05.560
Those low-resource languages

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.812
don't show up on the Internet 
as text as much

00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:11.024
which means they don't make it 
into language datasets.

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.943
They become unintelligible
to the AI.

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:15.987
Imagine our used bookstore again.

00:03:16.321 --> 00:03:18.281
It has a ton of Dan Brown books

00:03:18.281 --> 00:03:20.783
or James Patterson 
or Anne Tyler.

00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:23.286
This is like English and German 
and Chinese.

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:25.413
The high-resource languages.

00:03:25.413 --> 00:03:27.373
Then there are the rare books.

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.792
These are the low-resource languages.

00:03:30.293 --> 00:03:33.713
So, many models just don't know
as much about them...

00:03:33.922 --> 00:03:35.590
or have anything at all.

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.425
I’m someone from Jamaica.

00:03:37.425 --> 00:03:39.886
The language primarily spoken 
in Jamaica is English...

00:03:40.011 --> 00:03:43.514
but we also speak a Creole language 
called Jamaican patois.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.267
Armstrong and her coauthors wanted 
to create a dataset

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:49.604
that can explain this largely 
spoken language

00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:52.065
but they weren't trying to generate 
texts like ChatGPT.

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.609
Instead, they wanted their model 
to understand it.

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:57.362
In this case, to do that, 
Armstrong went through

00:03:57.362 --> 00:04:00.281
a bunch of examples of Jamaican patois 
and lined them up.

00:04:00.657 --> 00:04:01.866
Two columns.

00:04:01.866 --> 00:04:03.451
And she labeled whether the statements

00:04:03.451 --> 00:04:06.996
entailed or agreed, contradicted, 
or were neutral.

00:04:08.164 --> 00:04:09.916
You can try it 
in this one.

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:11.251
A has a fever.

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:12.877
B has a high temperature.

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:15.755
So it’s entailment.

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:16.923
They agree.

00:04:16.965 --> 00:04:19.801
Try this one: Entailment 
or contradiciton?

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:22.387
Contradiction.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:23.554
One more.

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:25.181
Neutral.

00:04:25.223 --> 00:04:27.350
The two statements 
don't really relate.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:31.354
She did that for almost 
650 examples.

00:04:31.396 --> 00:04:34.148
You can probably see that 
this was a ton of work.

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:37.902
And Jamaican patois is not on 
my big list of 

00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:39.821
Common Crawl languages.

00:04:40.571 --> 00:04:43.032
I also talked to some Catalan researchers...

00:04:43.241 --> 00:04:46.327
who are trying to evaluate how well 
these big language models

00:04:46.327 --> 00:04:48.663
do on stuff like Catalan.

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:53.167
It is the most spoken in this 
autonomous community of Spain.

00:04:53.209 --> 00:04:59.382
In GPT-3, the percentage 
of English words is 92%.

00:04:59.382 --> 00:05:03.136
For German, there's 1.4% words.

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:07.181
Spanish appears in 0.7%.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.100
And finally, Catalan.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:15.315
The amount of Catalan words in 
the whole training set is 0.01%.

00:05:15.523 --> 00:05:17.608
And it still performs very, very well.

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:20.737
So the problem here is 
a little bit different, right?

00:05:20.737 --> 00:05:23.740
They've got some Catalan 
in the dataset.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:28.161
Common Crawl says Catalan is 
0.2335% of their survey.

00:05:28.202 --> 00:05:30.204
Not a lot, but some.

00:05:30.204 --> 00:05:34.959
In the big company models like GPT-3 
and presumably GPT-4 in the future

00:05:34.959 --> 00:05:37.962
were proven to do pretty well 
on little data.

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:40.715
For example, the research team 
got GPT-3

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:43.259
to generate 3 Catalan sentences.

00:05:43.593 --> 00:05:45.762
And then they mixed them up 
with real sentences.

00:05:46.095 --> 00:05:48.431
Three native speakers 
then evaluated them.

00:05:48.431 --> 00:05:50.350
So, that was our test.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:53.102
And their results were very good 
for the machine.

00:05:54.062 --> 00:05:55.646
But there is still a catch.

00:05:55.646 --> 00:05:57.648
It performs reasonably well.

00:05:57.899 --> 00:06:01.444
But it's worth it to build 
a language-specific model

00:06:01.444 --> 00:06:05.198
that has been specifically trained
and evaluated for that language.

00:06:05.323 --> 00:06:07.575
So the problem here 
isn't performance

00:06:07.575 --> 00:06:10.536
it’s transparency and 
it's the amount of data.

00:06:10.578 --> 00:06:13.122
I mean, Common Crawl says 
that they indexed...

00:06:13.373 --> 00:06:16.084
millions of examples 
of Catalan words.

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:19.504
But GPT-3 says that they only read

00:06:19.504 --> 00:06:22.131
about 140 pages of Catalan.

00:06:22.131 --> 00:06:23.925
Imagine like a novella.

00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:26.052
It's a problem being dependent

00:06:26.094 --> 00:06:29.514
on the performance or 
even the goodwill

00:06:29.514 --> 00:06:32.767
of a few institutions or 
a few companies.

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:35.561
You can easily imagine a world where 
one of these companies

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:38.481
just cuts out Catalan.

00:06:38.773 --> 00:06:42.068
The same way Catalan News complained 
Google was cutting out

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:44.112
Catalan links in searches.

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:48.199
Common Crawl is just 
a percent of what

00:06:48.199 --> 00:06:49.951
GPT-3 was trained for.

00:06:49.951 --> 00:06:52.286
We don't know the details 
about GPT-4.

00:06:52.286 --> 00:06:54.247
And that means a lot of other stuff

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:57.333
went into this language model that 
we just don't know about.

00:06:57.375 --> 00:07:00.044
Right now all these bookstores 
are actually run by 

00:07:00.044 --> 00:07:03.506
Meta or Microsoft or Baidu 
or Open AI or Google.

00:07:03.965 --> 00:07:06.175
They decide which books 
go in there

00:07:06.175 --> 00:07:09.512
and don't tell anyone where they came from 
or who wrote them.

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:12.598
Some people are trying 
to build a library

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:13.808
next to the bookstore.

00:07:15.476 --> 00:07:18.855
This is Paris, where the French 
have a supercomputer

00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:20.231
that wasn't being used a lot.

00:07:20.231 --> 00:07:21.399
It’s like almost down the road

00:07:21.399 --> 00:07:23.526
and I was discussing with the people...

00:07:23.985 --> 00:07:26.696
who built it and they're like, 
“Nobody uses this GPU.”

00:07:26.696 --> 00:07:28.364
Basically, what can we do?

00:07:28.364 --> 00:07:30.825
Thomas Wolf is a co-founder 
of Hugging Face

00:07:30.825 --> 00:07:34.537
which is like a hub for AI research 
on the Internet

00:07:34.537 --> 00:07:37.248
and they ended up working 
on Big Science’s BLOOM...

00:07:37.582 --> 00:07:41.043
a project to create an open-source 
multilingual model.

00:07:41.043 --> 00:07:42.253
And the more we thought about it

00:07:42.253 --> 00:07:45.131
we thought it's also a lot better, 
in fact, that we trained it

00:07:45.131 --> 00:07:47.842
in a lot of other languages, 
not just English.

00:07:47.842 --> 00:07:49.719
And if we try to involve 
many people

00:07:49.719 --> 00:07:52.597
and so it started from a small 
Hugging Face project

00:07:52.597 --> 00:07:54.807
to become a very big collaboration.

00:07:55.183 --> 00:07:57.477
Where we tried to open this 
to everyone.

00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:59.353
They basically went down 
the Wikipedia list

00:07:59.353 --> 00:08:01.397
of most spoken languages
and covered those.

00:08:01.606 --> 00:08:04.567
But also added low-resource languages 
when possible.

00:08:04.734 --> 00:08:07.361
So we have very, very low-resource 
languages there.

00:08:07.361 --> 00:08:09.697
Mostly in African languages.

00:08:09.697 --> 00:08:11.908
And so here to gather 
the data there

00:08:11.908 --> 00:08:13.826
what we decided was to partner

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:15.828
as much as possible with 
local communities

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:18.581
and ask them basically what they thought 
were good data

00:08:18.581 --> 00:08:20.041
and how we could get it.

00:08:20.041 --> 00:08:22.919
As importantly, we know where 
the data comes from

00:08:22.919 --> 00:08:23.836
and how it was obtained.

00:08:24.253 --> 00:08:27.048
That's the difference in open-source.

00:08:27.256 --> 00:08:29.467
You know the books in the library.

00:08:30.885 --> 00:08:32.762
All right, let me find English.

00:08:32.762 --> 00:08:35.473
Okay, so let's being honest 
as an English speaker...

00:08:35.681 --> 00:08:37.266
I'm kind of the target audience

00:08:37.266 --> 00:08:39.519
for these big companies 
in these big models.

00:08:40.102 --> 00:08:41.437
English represents

00:08:41.437 --> 00:08:44.023
more than 40% of the 
Common Crawl

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:46.526
but there are reasons for even 
the target audience

00:08:46.526 --> 00:08:49.695
to want all languages to be 
well represented.

00:08:51.739 --> 00:08:54.700
I am an English speaker but 
I have my Jamaican accent

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.452
and I remember that...

00:08:56.702 --> 00:09:01.123
initially like when Siri came out, 
I had a harder time using it

00:09:01.123 --> 00:09:03.793
because it couldn't understand 
my accent.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:07.255
So expanding even 
the training dataset

00:09:07.255 --> 00:09:09.674
for voice assistants include...

00:09:09.966 --> 00:09:12.343
more accents has been helpful.

00:09:12.593 --> 00:09:13.761
So imagine what would happen

00:09:13.761 --> 00:09:16.097
if we tried to expand 
another piece of that.

00:09:16.097 --> 00:09:18.683
We're building technologies for 
more languages as well.

00:09:19.058 --> 00:09:21.477
So if you want to have 
this model everywhere

00:09:21.477 --> 00:09:23.020
you need to be able 
to trust them.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.523
So if you trust Microsoft, 
that's fine.

00:09:25.523 --> 00:09:26.774
But if you don't trust them...

00:09:27.817 --> 00:09:28.484
yeah.

00:09:28.943 --> 00:09:30.486
It's our language.

00:09:30.486 --> 00:09:32.947
So we speak— we are 
Catalan speakers.

00:09:33.489 --> 00:09:35.616
So whereas because of 
a small language

00:09:35.616 --> 00:09:37.535
or of a moderately 
small language

00:09:37.535 --> 00:09:40.371
because you may have languages 
that have a...

00:09:41.497 --> 00:09:44.125
a sizable amount of speakers 
in the real world

00:09:44.125 --> 00:09:46.919
but that have very, very little 
digital footprint.

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:49.171
So they are bound to just...

00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:50.381
disappear.

